---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import RecordMap from "../components/RecordMap.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import idx from "../../public/index.json";
import regions from "../../public/regions.json";

const places = idx.places.length;
const volumes = idx.places.reduce((a, p) => a + (p.c || 0), 0);
const countries = new Set(idx.places.map(p => p.k).filter(Boolean)).size;
---
<Base
  bare
  title="Record Atlas"
  dek="Every place your people came from, and who holds the paper.">

  <div class="pagehead">
    <div class="lab">{places} places · {volumes} record volumes · {countries} countries · {regions.regions.length} record regions</div>
    <h1>Where the records are</h1>
    <p class="dek">
      Genealogy is not short of websites. It is short of an answer to one question:
      <em>my people came from here — who holds the paper, and can I see it tonight?</em>
      This map answers that geographically. Click a place and it lists every collection
      that covers it, coloured by what it actually costs to look. Set a year and it also
      tells you whose empire that ground belonged to, and therefore whose archive to write to.
    </p>
  </div>

  <RecordMap
    indexUrl={u("/index.json")}
    detailBase={u("/p/")}
    regionsUrl={u("/regions.json")}
    providersUrl={u("/providers.json")} />

  <div class="one" style="margin-top:34px">
    <div class="lab">Why a map, and not another list</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p><b>Three things stand between a researcher and a record, and all three are geographic.</b></p>
      <p><b>The name changed.</b> Gallignana is Gračišće. Fiume is Rijeka. Pola is Pula.
      You cannot search for a place whose name you do not know, so the find box here matches
      every name a place has ever had — type <em>Gallignana</em> and the map goes to Gračišće.</p>
      <p><b>The jurisdiction changed.</b> An 1863 baptism from a Croatian village sits in a
      Croatian archive. A 1930 one, from the same village, is filed under <em>Italy</em> —
      because between the wars that village was Italian. Searching the Croatian collection
      alone finds nothing, and nothing is not the same as not existing. Set the year and this
      map tells you which it is.</p>
      <p><b>"Online" is four different things.</b> Free images, images behind a login, an index
      with no images at all, and a catalogue card for paper nobody has photographed. Most
      directories flatten these into a link. Here they are the colour of the dot.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="one" style="margin-top:26px;border-left-color:var(--rule-strong)">
    <div class="lab">What this map does not know yet</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>It is honest about being early. The shelf is deep for <b>Croatia, Istria and Carnia</b>
      — {places} places and {volumes} volumes — and the record regions cover the ground those
      places sit on. <b>Everywhere else is a provider list, not a shelf</b>, and a place outside
      a drawn region says so in its own panel rather than pretending.</p>
      <p><b>A grey dot means nobody has surveyed it. It does not mean nothing is there.</b>
      That distinction is the difference between a map and a claim, and it is worth more
      than coverage.</p>
      <p><a href={u("/add/")}>Add a source, or correct one →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
